  "live_records": {
    "q-107888": {
      "dom_id": "step-q107888",
      "name": "Question 1",
      "problem": "Which of the following integrals are equivalent to $Γ(z)$ for $\\text{Re}(z)>0?$\n\nI. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{t}^{z-1}}{{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the definition of the gamma function:\n\n$$\nΓ(z)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\text{Re}(z)>0.\n$$\n\nWith this definition in mind, let's consider each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I matches the definition:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t=Γ(z)\n$$\n\n- Integral II matches the definition:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{t}^{z-1}}{{e}^{t}}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t=Γ(z)\n$$\n\n- Integral III does *not* match the definition. The lower bound of integration does not match that given in the definition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
