    "q-107829": {
      "dom_id": "step-q107829",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Which of the following integrals are equivalent to $Γ(z)$ for $\\text{Re}(z)>0?$\n\nI. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{{t}^{1-z}}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{-1}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the definition of the gamma function:\n\n$$\nΓ(z)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\text{Re}(z)>0.\n$$\n\nWith this definition in mind, let's consider each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I matches the definition:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{{t}^{1-z}}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t=Γ(z)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Integral II does *not* match the definition. The lower bound of integration does not match that given in the definition.\n\n- Integral III does *not* match the definition. The integrand ${t}^{z-1}{e}^{t}$ is not equivalent to that shown in the definition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
