    "q-107838": {
      "dom_id": "step-q107838",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Which of the following integrals are equivalent to $Γ(z)$ for $\\text{Re}(z)>0?$\n\nI. $3{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{t}^{3z-1}}{{e}^{{t}^{3}}}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nII. $3{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{3z-3}{e}^{-{t}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nIII. $3{\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }{t}^{3z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the definition of the gamma function:\n\n$$\nΓ(z)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\text{Re}(z)>0.\n$$\n\nWith this definition in mind, let's consider each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I matches the definition. We use a substitution $s={t}^{3},$ then $3{t}^{2}\\,\\text{d}t=\\text{d}s.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{3z-1}{e}^{-{t}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t & =3{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{3(z-1)+2}{e}^{-{t}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{3(z-1)}{e}^{-{t}^{3}}(3{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{s}^{z-1}{e}^{-s}\\,\\text{d}s=Γ(z).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Integral II does *not* match the definition. The integrand ${t}^{3z-3}{e}^{-{t}^{3}}$ is not equivalent to that shown in the definition.\n\n- Integral III does *not* match the definition. The lower bound of integration does not match that given in the definition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
