    "q-107833": {
      "dom_id": "step-q107833",
      "name": "Question 4",
      "problem": "Which of the following integrals are equivalent to $Γ(z)$ for $\\text{Re}(z)>0?$\n\nI. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{e}^{-t}}{{t}^{z-1}}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nII. ${\\int }_{1}^{\\infty }\\frac{{t}^{z-1}}{{e}^{t}}\\,\\text{d}t$\n\nIII. ${\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}t}{{t}^{1-z}{e}^{t}}$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's recall the definition of the gamma function:\n\n$$\nΓ(z)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t,\\,\\text{Re}(z)>0.\n$$\n\nWith this definition in mind, let's consider each of the given integrals.\n\n- Integral I does *not* match the definition. The integrand $\\frac{{e}^{t}}{{t}^{z-1}}$ is not equivalent to that shown in the definition.\n\n- Integral II does *not* match the definition.  The lower bound of integration does not match that given in the definition.\n\n- Integral III matches the definition:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{\\text{d}t}{{t}^{1-z}{e}^{t}}={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{z-1}{e}^{-t}\\,\\text{d}t=Γ(z)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
          "choices": [
            {
              "option": "a",
              "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-107833-a",
              "type": "text",
